  "id": "COMPOUND:12208 - PROTEIN:772",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanistic link between the Influenza B virus B/Hong Kong/259/2010 hemagglutinin antigen and C-X-C chemokine receptor type 4, mediated through the target of Magnesium levulinate. This relationship is supported by multiple paths, all of which indicate that the hemagglutinin antigen acts as a target for a compound that subsequently targets C-X-C chemokine receptor type 4. The shared involvement of Magnesium levulinate as a common target across different cellular components (apolipoprotein L1, alkaline phosphatase, and serum amyloid P-component) suggests a broader biological role for this compound in modulating chemokine signaling pathways. The presence of a consistent and high-scoring pathway across three distinct paths strengthens the hypothesis, though the exact mechanism by which Magnesium levulinate influences C-X-C chemokine receptor type 4 remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a plausible, though not yet fully understood, pathway where the hemagglutinin antigen interacts with a compound that modulates chemokine receptor activity.",
